--[[
	THE ASSOCIATION ARCHIVE: lore entries (kept across lives). { id, category, title, text, secret }
	Each says what it is: [CANON] (the manhwa), or [GAME] (this game's own rule or number).
	Facts follow docs/BITLEVELING_CANON.md; uncertain ones are worded as uncertain.
]]

return {
	-- ── Gates and Dungeons ──
	{ id = "lore_gates", category = "gates", title = "Gates", text = "[CANON] About ten years before the story begins, Gates started opening all over the world: tears in the air, leading to dungeons full of monsters. Every Gate has a rank, E, D, C, B, A or S, from the strength of the mana it gives off. The Hunter Association measures it and sells the right to raid it.\n\n[GAME] Gates here are not a menu. The world makes its own, in streets, stations, forests and farms, and runs each one through its life: it opens, someone notices (or doesn't), the Association responds, a raid goes in, and it's cleared or it breaks." },
	{ id = "lore_ranks", category = "gates", title = "Ranks", text = "[CANON] Hunters and Gates share six ranks: E, D, C, B, A, S. A Hunter's rank is measured when they awaken and, almost always, it stays the same for life. S-rank Hunters are very rare: in canon, Korea recognised only ten in the whole story.\n\n[CANON] There's no colour code for Gate ranks. Ordinary Gates look blue. Only a Red Gate is named for its colour, and it turns red after the raid has gone in.\n\n[GAME] Rank mismatch is real here. An E-rank Hunter in front of an A-rank monster will almost certainly die, and the odds on the card will tell you so." },
	{ id = "lore_breaks", category = "gates", title = "Dungeon Breaks", text = "[CANON] A Gate that isn't cleared in time breaks: its monsters come out into the world. The figure people cite is about seven days, but in canon an S-rank Gate in Tokyo broke after about three. Treat seven days as typical, not a promise.\n\n[GAME] A Gate nobody finds (deep in a forest, up a mountain) is the most dangerous kind: it's discovered by the people its monsters reach first." },
	{ id = "lore_crystals", category = "gates", title = "Mana Crystals", text = "[CANON] Monsters carry mana crystals, and dungeons hold deposits of them. They're a Hunter's main income, an energy source and a crafting material. Electronics fail inside dungeons, so mining crews dig by hand with pickaxes. Guilds employ porters and miners; rare loot goes to auction.\n\n[CANON, likely] A Gate closes about an hour after its boss dies, so collection teams work fast." },
	{ id = "lore_red", category = "gates", title = "Red Gates", text = "[CANON] A Red Gate looks ordinary until the raid is inside. Then it turns red and the way out disappears. Nobody can get in, nobody can get out, until the boss dies. The reading taken before entry is wrong: in canon's Red Gate it said C, and what was inside was far worse.\n\n[CANON, likely] Time runs differently inside: about a day for every hour outside.\n\n[GAME] Red Gates are rare here. When one happens near you, you'll know." },
	{ id = "lore_double", category = "gates", title = "Double Dungeons", text = "[CANON] A Double Dungeon is a second, hidden dungeon inside an ordinary one. The only canon case, the Cartenon Temple inside a D-rank Gate, killed most of the raid party that found it.\n\n[GAME] Double Dungeons here are vanishingly rare, never repeatable, and never the Cartenon Temple. What chose Sung Jinwoo chose him; nothing in a Double Dungeon chooses you." },
	{ id = "lore_rights", category = "gates", title = "Raid Rights", text = "[CANON, likely] The Association detects and measures Gates, then sells the right to raid them to guilds and parties. Big guilds can be required to take emergency Gates, in return for tax breaks.\n\n[GAME] Going into a Gate you don't hold the rights to is against the law here. People still do it." },

	-- ── Hunters ──
	{ id = "lore_awakening", category = "hunters", title = "Awakening", text = "[CANON] After the Gates came, some people awakened: they gained mana and abilities, and became Hunters. It seems random and can happen at any age. Awakened Hunters register with their country's Association, are tested, and receive a licence.\n\n[GAME] Most lives here never awaken. The odds by age, the rank you awaken at and the class are this game's numbers, because canon gives none." },
	{ id = "lore_classes", category = "hunters", title = "Hunter Classes", text = "[CANON] Hunters are described by class: Fighters, Mages, Assassins, Tankers, Rangers and Healers. A raid party needs all of them: Tankers in front, Healers behind, everyone else somewhere in between.\n\n[GAME] The class you awaken with leans your stats; it doesn't stop you from becoming anything else." },
	{ id = "lore_reawakening", category = "hunters", title = "Reawakening", text = "[CANON] Rank is normally fixed. A reawakening, a second awakening that raises a Hunter's rank, is extremely rare, rare enough that when an E-rank came back from a reassessment as an S-rank, the Association assumed that's what it was.\n\n[GAME] A life here can reawaken, once, by a very long chance." },
	{ id = "lore_fear", category = "hunters", title = "Fear", text = "[GAME] Hunters freeze in front of things that are stronger than them. Here, Willpower is how much fear a mind can carry, and Composure is how much is left. A shaken Hunter loses clashes they should win." },
	{ id = "lore_system", category = "hunters", title = "The System", text = "[CANON] One Hunter had a System: a game-like window only he could see, that let him grow stronger without limit. It was built to raise a vessel for the Shadow Monarch, and it chose Sung Jinwoo.\n\n[GAME] Nobody else has one. Not you either. Hunters here get stronger the way real people do: training, experience, and the limits of their rank." },

	-- ── Associations and Guilds ──
	{ id = "lore_kha", category = "orgs", title = "The Korean Hunters Association", text = "[CANON] Korea's Association licenses Hunters, measures Gates and sells raid rights. Its chairman through the story is Go Gunhee. Woo Jinchul, an A-rank, heads the Surveillance Team that polices Hunter crime, and later leads the Association." },
	{ id = "lore_guilds", category = "orgs", title = "Korea's Guilds", text = "[CANON] The Hunters Guild is the largest, the only one with two S-ranks: master Choi Jong-In, a fire mage, and vice-master Cha Hae-In, a swordswoman. The White Tiger Guild's master is Baek Yoonho, an S-rank who fights in the shape of a beast. The Fame Guild's Ma Dongwook, an S-rank Tanker, holds the Honam region. Late in the story Sung Jinwoo founds the Ahjin Guild, with Yoo Jinho as vice-master.\n\n[GAME] The guilds you'll actually work for are small and mid-size ones: this game's own." },
	{ id = "lore_abroad", category = "orgs", title = "Abroad", text = "[CANON] In the United States, the Hunter Bureau is a federal agency; it hosts the yearly International Guild Conference. The Scavenger Guild, the strongest in America, is led by Thomas Andre. In Japan, the Draw Sword Guild's Goto Ryuji was the country's strongest Hunter, and the Japanese Hunters Association's chairman was Matsumoto Shigeo." },
	{ id = "lore_national", category = "orgs", title = "National Level Hunters", text = "[CANON] Five Hunters survived the fight against the first S-rank Dungeon Break, a dragon on the US West Coast, and became the National Level Hunters: each one worth as much as a country's army. Four are named: Thomas Andre and Christopher Reed of the United States, Liu Zhigang of China, and Siddharth Bachchan of India. The fifth is never named in the original story." },

	-- ── What Happened ──
	{ id = "lore_timeline", category = "history", title = "The Years (relative)", text = "[CANON, years approximate] Year 0: the first Gates. About Year 2: the dragon on the US West Coast, and the National Level Hunters. About Year 6: Jeju Island falls to an S-rank break. Years 7 to 9: raids to take it back fail. About Year 8: Cha Hae-In, Korea's 9th S-rank. Year 10: the story begins; Korea registers a 10th S-rank. After: Jeju retaken, Tokyo's giants, Christopher Reed's death, the chairman's death, the Gate over Seoul, and the war.\n\n[CANON] Canon gives no calendar year for any of it. Here, years are counted from the first Gates." },
	{ id = "lore_jeju", category = "history", title = "Jeju Island", text = "[CANON] About four years before the story began, an S-rank Gate opened on Jeju Island. The raid failed, the Gate broke, and giant ants overran the island; it was abandoned. Three raids to retake it failed. The fourth, a joint Korean and Japanese raid, killed the Ant King. Eight S-ranks died in it." },
	{ id = "lore_tokyo", category = "history", title = "Tokyo", text = "[CANON] Weeks after Jeju, an S-rank Gate opened in Tokyo. Japan hired foreign S-ranks; a barrier that was meant to hold the Gate failed, and giants walked out into the city. A single Hunter from Korea is credited with stopping them." },
	{ id = "lore_war", category = "history", title = "The War", text = "[CANON] At the end of the story, Gates open everywhere at once, and armies come through them, led by beings the Hunters had no name for. Hunters all over the world fight. The war ends in the final battle.", secret = true },
	{ id = "lore_rewrite", category = "history", title = "The Rewritten World", text = "[CANON] After the war, time was turned back about ten years, to before the Gates, and the war was fought out of sight, where nobody on Earth would see it. In the new world the Gates never opened. Nobody remembers.\n\n[GAME] If you let it happen, your life goes on in that world: no Gates, no Hunters, no rank. (Turning your own years back isn't simulated; you go on from where you are.) You can also choose to stay on a world line where the Gates never closed. That line is not canon, and the game says so.", secret = true },

	-- ── Beyond ──
	{ id = "lore_monarchs", category = "beyond", title = "Monarchs", text = "[CANON] Nine Monarchs, each a king of a race of monsters: Ashborn the Shadow Monarch; Antares, Monarch of Destruction, King of Dragons; Querehsha, Queen of Insects; Rakan, King of Beasts; Baran, Monarch of White Flames, King of Demons; Yogumunt, Monarch of Transfiguration; Legia, Monarch of the Beginning, King of Giants; Tarnak, Monarch of Iron Body; and Sillad, the Frost Monarch, King of the Snow Folk.\n\n[GAME] Nobody in your life knows these names. You do, because you're reading the archive.", secret = true },
	{ id = "lore_rulers", category = "beyond", title = "Rulers", text = "[CANON] The Rulers are beings of light, enemies of the Monarchs, who act through human hosts. Their war is older than the Earth's Gates, and the Gates are part of it.", secret = true },

	-- ── how the game works ──
	{ id = "lore_game_modes", category = "game", title = "Two Worlds", text = "[GAME] The Normal World is the story's world: Gates are dangerous and breaks happen, but help usually comes. The High-Danger World is unstable: more Gates open in streets, more break, the Association is stretched thin, help is late or doesn't come, and a bad year can end a life. You pick one on the start screen, for the whole life." },
	{ id = "lore_game_news", category = "game", title = "The News", text = "[GAME] Everything the world does that you're not there for reaches you the way it reaches anyone: the news. Local incidents, national headlines, the world. The canon story's public events are on it, when they happen. Secret things never are." },
}
